  {
    "episode_id": 86579,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/6/86579.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 52,
    "turn_text": "Here's what I say as a constitutional lawyer and someone who has litigated in the course. I mean, the first thing is Roe v. Wade is currently on the books. It's going to be for the foreseeable future. The first thing we need to do, those of us who are pro-life, and I'm 100% pro-life, have been my whole life. Those of us who are pro-life need to work and fight for laws that the public approves of that can expand pro-life protections. Right now in this state, my office as Attorney General, we're defending three major lawsuits with Missouri's pro-life laws, laws that impose common sense health and safety regulations on abortion clinics. laws that require abortion clinics to have emergency plans, laws that require doctors to have admitting privileges, and so forth. These, I think, are widely supported by the public. Their common sense. Take recent bills that would prevent abortion when the infant is capable of pain. That's something where I think broad swaths of the American public, certainly Missourians, agree on that. Let's start there, get public consensus, and work forward.",
