    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/6/86579.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 78,
    "turn_text": "Well, I did not see the press conference. I read the transcripts later. But look, I think here's the thing at the bottom line on Russia. You've got an administration that is pressing them on some points that is holding out options for them to say, if you are willing to come to the table, I thought that the president pressing Angela Merkel of Germany on Germany and other members of the European Union buying so much natural gas from Russia. It's a really interesting point because that is, that gets at Russia's economic self-interest. And here you have the president saying, why are you doing this? Why are you buying natural gas from them and not from us? So at the end of the day, I think Russia has got to be treated as the competitor on the international stage like they are. They've got to be pressed to not to behave and not to interfere in our country and to observe democratic norms. But let me say something that needs to be said in this context is that the media fixation on all of this Russia talk, I think we have to admit stems from the fact that many, many people still think in the media and on the Democratic Party, including Claire McCaskill apparently, that Russia somehow stole the 2016 presidential election. There's no evidence to suggest that. That's just loony. I mean, Claire McCaskill told a reporter, Michael Isekoff, that the effing Russians were responsible for Donald Trump's victory. Now, that's ridiculous. If she actually said that, that's totally ridiculous. And I think this is why when I talk to voters, they don't pay a lot of attention to what's going on here because they think that this is a distraction from key issues that matter to them in their lives.",
